  "result_text": "**Recommendation: hold, unless your team already puts the odds of a real regression below about 2% before looking at this evidence.** The formal verdict is `prior-sensitive`, so the record returns the final call to you. The full record is at `work/decision-record.md`.\n\n**Setup.** The question is whether the checkout p95 regression is real served latency (the ledger's H1). The alternative is that it's a measurement artifact from the exporter (H2) or some explanation nobody named. Missing a real regression costs about 10 times a needless one-week hold, which I recorded as a loss ratio of 10 and a hold threshold of 0.1 posterior odds. Hold wins whenever the posterior odds of a real regression exceed 0.1, about 9%.\n\n**Evidence.** The ledger's \"UNRESOLVED\" tags carry no likelihood information, so I didn't use them. The only usable number is the reference class (T3). Past rollouts showed a gap over 40 ms in 9 of 10 real regressions and 2 of 10 non-regressions, so seeing the gap multiplies the odds of a real regression by 4.5. That ratio comes from only 20 rollouts, and those were drawn in fixed quotas, so it says nothing about how common regressions are. The 57 ms canary/control gap is counted once, through that ratio, not multiplied in a second time.\n\n**No base rate exists.** The ledger gives no supported prior, so I swept prior odds from 0.01 to 1 (about 1% to 50% chance of a real regression), all hypothetical. Over that range the posterior odds run from 0.045 to 4.5.\n\n**Where the answer flips.** The crossover is at prior odds of about 0.022, or roughly 2.2% before seeing the gap.\n- **Prior above about 2.2%:** hold.\n- **Prior below about 2.2%:** ship.\n\nThat is a very skeptical prior for a pool change that coincided with a 57 ms rise on every canary host. This is my reading, not something the data settles.\n\n**Fragility.** If the 4.5 ratio is overstated, for example 1.5 given the small samples, the crossover rises to about 6%. Hold is still favored for almost any non-skeptical prior. That figure is informational and not part of the swept class.\n\n**What would settle it.** Both explanations are unresolved only because the raw access logs (S3) haven't been restored. Recomputing p95 from them (T2) directly separates a real regression from an exporter artifact. If the restore can land before Friday, it's worth more than any prior debate. Compare its delay cost against the one-week hold. I haven't authorized or run anything; whether to pursue the restore is yours to decide."
